  saved: results/08_simulation/power_curve.png
  saved: results/08_simulation/fdr_curve.png

Takeaways: with clean cell lines even n=2-3 finds most changes. With
 tissue-level variability the same n misses a large share, and only more
 replicates recover it. Estimate YOUR BCV (module 05) before choosing n.
 Check the FDR column too: edgeR-QL and voom stay near 5%, while DESeq2's
 Wald test can run above the nominal FDR with few, noisy replicates (QL methods
 are deliberately more conservative). Exact numbers move between runs -- change
 the seed or add reps to see how much. Real data has outliers and batch effects
 the simulator lacks, so this is the BEST case.

DESeq2 sensitivity by true |log2FC| (n=3, tissue-level variability):
[0.5,1] (1,1.5] (1.5,2]   (2,3] (3,Inf] 
   0.11    0.25    0.49    0.71    1.00 

======================================================================
  4. Pitfall: technical replicates are not biological replicates
======================================================================
Mock comparison, technical runs as samples:  3148 'DE' genes
 Same data, runs summed per donor (collapseReplicates): 55 
 Technical replicates only measure sequencing noise, so the model thinks the
 data are far more precise than they are. Sum them; never count them as n.

======================================================================
  5. Pitfall: double dipping
======================================================================
True DE genes: 0. Smallest padj: 0.019 
  saved: results/08_simulation/double_dipping_heatmap.png
The heatmap clusters the samples perfectly by group -- from noise. Genes
 SELECTED for separating the groups will always separate the groups. Such a
 heatmap illustrates a result; it is never evidence for one.
